# Wayfinder environment variables.
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Every one of these is OPTIONAL — with none set,
# the app runs in local (device-only) mode: no database, no AI, no address
# search, no sign-in. The map still fetches street tiles from CARTO's CDN and
# Google Fonts at build time; nothing about the user leaves the device except
# those tile requests.

# ── Public origin ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The URL people reach the site at, e.g. https://wayfinder.example.nhs.uk. Used
# for absolute URLs in link previews (Open Graph), the sitemap and robots.txt.
# On Vercel and Railway it is inferred from the host's own variables when unset;
# elsewhere it falls back to http://localhost:3000. Share links and QR posters
# use the browser's own origin and do not need this.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ── Backend: pooled navigation signals + search misses + shared venues (Railway Postgres) ──
# Railway's Postgres plugin exposes two connection strings. The app reads either.
#   • On Railway: DATABASE_URL (the private …​.railway.internal host).
#   • On Vercel (or anywhere reaching Railway over the internet): DATABASE_PUBLIC_URL.
# Set whichever matches where the app runs. TLS is turned on automatically for a
# remote host, so ?sslmode=require is not required in the string.
DATABASE_URL=
DATABASE_PUBLIC_URL=

# ── AI sign-reading in Survey Mode (server-side only) ────────────────────────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── Real outdoor routing + place search (server-side only) ───────────────────
MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── Optional accounts (Clerk) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-in is deliberately optional and additive. Wayfinder works fully
# anonymously — you never have to sign in to find a ward, the same way Google
# Maps and Apple Maps let you navigate signed out. An account only carries the
# things that must follow a *person* rather than a device: saved places, and
# ownership of a venue you mapped.
#
# With these unset, ClerkProvider is not mounted (src/app/layout.tsx) and
# src/proxy.ts is a pass-through, so the app behaves exactly as it always has.
#
# Note src/proxy.ts deliberately EXCLUDES /navigate from the auth matcher: that
# is the route used inside hospitals where signal dies, and it must never depend
# on an auth round-trip.
#
# Keys: https://dashboard.clerk.com/ → your app → API keys
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Back office (/admin) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Managing Findway: venues, coverage, missed searches, activity, accounts and
# roles. Needs the Clerk keys above and the database. docs/backoffice.md.
#
# Email addresses of the owners, comma separated. Verified addresses only; these
# owners cannot be demoted or removed from the back office.
ADMIN_EMAILS=
# Optional: a fine-grained GitHub token with Actions read and write on this
# repository, so admins can start a world data refresh from the back office.
GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN=
# Development only: with this set to 1 under `next dev`, the back office opens
# as an owner without signing in. Ignored by production builds.
BACKOFFICE_DEV=

# ── Native apps (build time only) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Android and iOS apps bundle the pages and call a deployment's /api. Set
# this before `npm run mobile:build`; CI defaults it to the production site.
# See docs/mobile.md.
FINDWAY_API_URL=

# The same DATABASE_URL also holds the worldwide hospital data (lists, indoor
# and site maps from OpenStreetMap) that /api/world/* serves; see
# docs/world-data.md. The World hospital data workflow needs it as a repository
# secret to publish.
